## Set realistic goals

Aim for **slow, steady change**. Losing around 0.5kg to 1kg (1 to 2 pounds) a week is a realistic goal. Even small losses bring health benefits.

## Eating well

- base meals on **vegetables, fruit and wholegrains**
- include **protein**, such as beans, lentils, fish, eggs or lean meat, which helps you feel full
- watch **portion sizes**. Using a smaller plate can help
- cut down on **sugary drinks**, and swap to water or sugar-free drinks
- plan meals ahead so you are less likely to reach for convenience food
- the free **NHS Weight Loss Plan app** can help you track your progress

## Moving more

Any movement counts, and it helps your health whether or not you lose weight.

- start with **what you can do**, even a 10 minute walk
- try **swimming, water aerobics or cycling**, which are easier on the joints
- chair-based exercises are a good start if moving is hard
- build up slowly towards **150 minutes a week**

## Sleep and stress

Poor sleep and stress can affect your appetite. Aim for a regular sleep routine. If you snore loudly, stop breathing briefly in your sleep, or feel very tired during the day, ask your GP about **sleep apnoea**.

## Look after your mental health

Weight stigma is real and can be hurtful. You deserve to be treated with respect, including by health services. If you are struggling with your mood or your relationship with food, talk to your GP. **Beat** (0808 801 0677) supports people with eating disorders.

## Everyday aids

If your weight makes some tasks harder, aids such as a **long-handled sponge**, **grabber**, **sock aid**, **bath lift** or **stronger chair** may help. Ask your council for a free needs assessment.
